# 2.2. Animal Studies

2.2. Animal StudiesC57BL/6J wild type (WT) mice were purchased from Jackson Laboratories (Bar Harbor, ME, USA). \(\mathrm{Mdr}2^{-/-}\) mice with C57BL/6J background were a gift from Dr. Daniel Goldenberg at the Department of Pathology, Hadassah-Habrew University Medical Center, Jerusalem, Israel. Maternal \(\mathrm{H19}^{\Delta\mathrm{Exon1/+}}\) (H19KO) mice with C57BL/6J background were generated by Dr. Karl Pfeifer at NIH and provided by Dr. Jian-Ying Wang at the University of Maryland (Baltimore, MD, USA). \(\mathrm{Mdr}2^{-/-}\mathrm{H19}^{\mathrm{maternal}\Delta\mathrm{Exon1/+}}\) (DKO) mice were generated, as described previously [9]. WT, H19KO, \(\mathrm{Mdr}2^{-/-}\) mice, and DKO mice (both male and female) at 100-day old were used. For the bile duct ligation (BDL) mouse model, WT and H19KO mice (both male and female) at 12 weeks old were subjected to 2 weeks BDL or sham operation. All mice were housed under \(12\,\mathrm{h}/12\,\mathrm{h}\) light and dark cycle with free access to water and standard chow ad libitum. All animal experiments were performed following institutional guidelines for ethical animal studies and approved by the VCU institutional animal care and use committee (Approved protocol number: AD1001773).

# 2.3. Human Liver Samples

Frozen human liver tissues from healthy controls, PSC, and PBC patients were obtained through the liver tissue cell distribution system (Minneapolis, MN, USA), which is funded by the National Institutes of Health (contract no. HSN276201200017C).

2.4. Isolation and Culture of Primary Kupffer Cells and Bone Marrow-Derived Macrophages (BMDMs)

2.4. Isolation and Culture of Primary Kupffer Cells and Bone Marrow-Derived Macrophages (BMDMs)Primary mouse Kupffer cells from WT and H19KO mice were isolated using a two-step collagenase digestion method and cultured, as previously described [10]. Kupffer cells were washed with cold PBS to remove dead cells. The medium was changed every 3 days after plating. For primary BMDM isolation, mouse femurs were flushed with Dulbecco's modified eagle medium (DMEM) with penicillin G \((100\;\mathrm{U/mL})\) and streptomycin \((100\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) two times. Bone marrow cells were then seeded and cultured in DMEM containing \(10\%\) fetal bovine serum (FBS), penicillin G \((100\;\mathrm{U/mL})\), streptomycin \((100\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\), and \(10\%\) L-cell medium for 7 days. Adherent cells were washed and cultured with macrophage medium supplemented with \(10\,\mathrm{ng/mL}\) granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) (differentiation medium) to allow differentiation for another 7 days.

# 2.5. Cell Culture

2.5. Cell CultureMouse large cholangiocytes (MLE) were originally obtained from Dr. Gianfranco Alpini (Indiana University School of Medicine, Indianapolis, IN, USA) and were cultured with minimum essential medium (MEM) containing \(10\%\) FBS, penicillin G \((100\;\mathrm{U/mL})\), and streptomycin \((100\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\), as described previously [10]. All cells were cultured at \(37\,^{\circ}C\) with \(5\%\,{\cal C O}_{2}\) in a humidified cell culture incubator.

# 2.6. Cell Culture and Treatment

2.6. Cell Culture and TreatmentFor the study of lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation, Kupffer cells were cultured and treated with control exosomes, H19-overexpression exosomes with or without LPS \((10\,\mathrm{ng/mL})\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). For the study of BMDM polarization, BMDMs were cultured and differentiated for 14 days followed by treatment with M1 stimulators (LPS, \(10\ \mathrm{ng/mL}\) and \(\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma\), \(100\ \mathrm{ng/mL}\)) or M2 stimulators (IL-4, \(20\ \mathrm{ng/mL}\) and IL-13, \(20\ \mathrm{ng/mL}\)) for another \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). For the study of BMDM differentiation, primary BMDMs were isolated and cultured for 7 days. WT and H19KO BMDMs were cultured using fresh differentiation medium with control or H19-overexpression exosomes. The culture medium was collected on day 9 and 12. On day 14, BMDMs were treated with control or H19-overexpression exosomes and M1 stimulators for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
